{"agent":"fleet-hookid","data":{"by":"k3","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":2,"detail":"Under the default `lapsed_lease = \"accept\"` policy, the filter `policy == \"accept\" or lease.holder == me` accepts a lapsed lease for ANY committer identity `me`, not just the holder or the agent in the item's own tree. Both the function's docstring (\"refusing the holder, or the agent in the item's own tree\") and the new golden doc (\"lets the commit stand ... for the holder and for whoever commits in the item's own worktree\") say acceptance is restricted to those two identities. Concrete scenario: agent `alpha` claims an item with glob `src/**`, the lease lapses, and unrelated agent `beta` on a different machine/tree stages `src/foo.py`. `beta` is not the holder and has no lease in that tree, so `beta`'s paths are uncovered; `_lapsed_mine` returns alpha's lapsed lease (nothing live overlaps), and `_accepted_lapsed` accepts it because policy is `\"accept\"`, so `_after_accepting` empties `uncovered` and the hook returns `Verdict(OK, ...)`. The lease exclusivity the hook exists to enforce is thus void against any third party merely because the original holder forgot to heartbeat — the very bug the `holder` vs `accept` distinction in the doc says should not happen.","digest":"cbdbe11312ec8953","location":"ddflow/services/enforce.py:_accepted_lapsed","severity":"HIGH","title":"ddflow/services/enforce.py:_accepted_lapsed"}],"chunks_total":7,"coverage":"7/7 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":890087,"diff_sha":"29b6541477f227c9b7bb7e5faa8669da851623173345d9489873c24771519289","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":21.6,"family":"moonshot","findings":1,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"k3","output_digest":"9bc83565300f1b33","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/fix-B8aa03801a0.critic.kimi-k3.20261016T060837Z-9604e471.jsonl","reason":"","review_kind":